<h2>Who a ticket partner program is for</h2>
<p>Marketplaces work with different kinds of organizations, and each has its own questions. A ticket partner program on Seat Outlet is a conversation for any of these groups.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Venues:</strong> stadiums, arenas, theaters and clubs that want more visibility for open dates.</li>
  <li><strong>Festivals and promoters:</strong> organizers who need buyers beyond their own mailing list.</li>
  <li><strong>Teams and performers:</strong> groups that want a trusted place for fans to find tickets.</li>
  <li><strong>Brands and sponsors:</strong> companies exploring live-event partnerships.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/about-seat-outlet">about Seat Outlet page</a> describes the marketplace you would be joining, including the categories we list: concerts, sports, theater and festivals.</p>

<h3>How a ticket partner program helps your events</h3>
<p>A marketplace is a place where many sellers and buyers meet. The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Online_marketplace" target="_blank" rel="noopener">overview of online marketplaces on Wikipedia</a> explains the general model. For a live event, that can mean a wider audience and an easier search for fans, which is why a ticket partner program is worth a look when you want more people to find your dates.</p>

<h2>Questions to ask before you join any ticket partner program</h2>
<p>A good partnership is built on clear answers. Before you sign with us or anyone else, ask for the following in writing.</p>
<ol>
  <li>How are prices set, and who controls them?</li>
  <li>What fees apply, and who pays them?</li>
  <li>How and when are you paid?</li>
  <li>What happens if an event is canceled, postponed or rescheduled?</li>
  <li>How are buyer problems handled, and what is promised to fans?</li>
  <li>Who is the day-to-day contact on your account?</li>
</ol>

